Let a host choose the language and theme with ?lang= and ?theme=

An app embedding OpenMAIC in an iframe currently has no way to tell it which
language or theme to use. Both are read once on mount from bare localStorage
keys, which an embedder can only write by sharing an origin with it — and
sharing an origin is exactly the arrangement an embedder should not be pushed
into, since it also merges every other storage key and collapses the two apps
into one browser security principal.

Two query parameters close that gap without any of it:

  ?lang=th        resolved through the existing resolveLocale, so a host may
                  pass a loose code ('th', 'pt') without knowing the exact
                  locale identifier, and anything unrecognised falls back
                  rather than erroring
  ?theme=dark     validated against the same light/dark/system list already
                  used for the stored value

Precedence is query > stored > browser/system, and a host-supplied value is
persisted so it survives in-app navigation, which does not carry the query
along. With no query present nothing changes for anyone.

In use-i18n the query is read outside the try block on purpose: location.search
cannot throw, and a browser with storage blocked should still honour the host's
choice of language rather than losing it with the failed read.

Verified against a dev server with localStorage cleared first, so the query is
demonstrably what applied it: loading /?lang=th&theme=dark comes up in Thai with
the dark class set, and locale/theme are persisted as 'th-TH' and 'dark'.

Unrelated and left alone: app/layout.tsx hardcodes <html lang="en">, so the
document language attribute does not follow the resolved locale. Fixing that
needs locale detection to move server-side and is a separate change.

